1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,441
Hey everyone! Sometimes we need to know ...

2
00:00:02,441 --> 00:00:05,252
if the move that we want to make is worthwhile.

3
00:00:05,252 --> 00:00:08,285
While we don't have a time machine to peer into the ...

4
00:00:08,285 --> 00:00:10,356
future, we can use experiments to ...

5
00:00:10,356 --> 00:00:13,833
help us better understand if some initial change has the ...

6
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:15,756
impact that we are looking for.

7
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:17,240
Why do we care?

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,720
We are in the business of, well,

9
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:24,287
being in business and making the right decisions ...

10
00:00:24,287 --> 00:00:25,879
can improve our outcomes.

11
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,713
This is where having a well-designed experiment ...

12
00:00:29,713 --> 00:00:32,711
really ups the ante by allowing us to make causal ...

13
00:00:32,711 --> 00:00:34,884
inferences into business policy,

14
00:00:34,884 --> 00:00:36,158
changes, et cetera.

15
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,512
We gain functional knowledge that reduces ...

16
00:00:39,512 --> 00:00:40,755
uncertainty. This,

17
00:00:40,755 --> 00:00:43,679
then leads to information that we may use later.

18
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:45,971
A simple question. We know,

19
00:00:45,971 --> 00:00:48,304
or will know, that if we know the price ...

20
00:00:48,304 --> 00:00:51,616
elasticity of demand for a particular product we can ...

21
00:00:51,616 --> 00:00:54,251
raise or lower price to increase revenue.

22
00:00:54,251 --> 00:00:57,187
But our goal may not always be to raise revenue.

23
00:00:57,187 --> 00:00:59,520
Perhaps, you're trying to align with ...

24
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:02,907
another organizational goal of maximizing profits,

25
00:01:02,907 --> 00:01:06,294
in which case continually increasing revenue has the ...

26
00:01:06,294 --> 00:01:07,875
downfall of added costs.

27
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:11,176
What to do? Simply conduct a randomized ...

28
00:01:11,176 --> 00:01:14,398
experiment to find out. This is where the tricky part ...

29
00:01:14,398 --> 00:01:16,595
comes in. You have to be able to set up ...

30
00:01:16,595 --> 00:01:19,451
the experiment so randomization of consumers ...

31
00:01:19,451 --> 00:01:21,794
are not related in any systematic way.

32
00:01:21,794 --> 00:01:25,089
Suppose you offer a voucher to the first 100 people who ...

33
00:01:25,089 --> 00:01:27,652
enter your store on Tuesday. Why Tuesday?

34
00:01:27,652 --> 00:01:28,677
Why not Tuesday?

35
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,289
What will we notice? That there are 100 motivated ...

36
00:01:32,289 --> 00:01:34,474
people looking to get the voucher.

37
00:01:34,474 --> 00:01:36,809
This is not randomization. Perhaps,

38
00:01:36,809 --> 00:01:40,124
you have a machine that distributes vouchers using a ...

39
00:01:40,124 --> 00:01:43,439
random number generator. The first person who gets a ...

40
00:01:43,439 --> 00:01:45,322
voucher is customer number 3,

41
00:01:45,322 --> 00:01:47,733
then next person is customer number 5,

42
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:49,918
then the next is customer number 9.

43
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:52,630
With this method, we can see if the vouchers ...

44
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:56,322
have any effect on shopping. This also lets us potentially ...

45
00:01:56,322 --> 00:01:59,863
avoid selection bias.With our first proposed method of ...

46
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:03,555
giving out the vouchers to the first 100 people we create an ...

47
00:02:03,555 --> 00:02:06,870
incentive for the highly motivated to be at the store ...

48
00:02:06,870 --> 00:02:09,356
first. This distorts the true effect of ...

49
00:02:09,356 --> 00:02:11,993
the treatment of our experiment, or goal.

50
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:16,013
Here we have our store with an end cap display that is ...

51
00:02:16,013 --> 00:02:17,513
30%. We did this because an ...

52
00:02:17,513 --> 00:02:20,241
eager young assistant manager promised that we ...

53
00:02:20,241 --> 00:02:23,037
could increase sales of the items nearby as well.

54
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:27,418
If this endcap is not displayed in an ad and only customers ...

55
00:02:27,418 --> 00:02:29,575
who enter the store know about it,

56
00:02:29,575 --> 00:02:32,811
then we have added some degree of randomness to the ...

57
00:02:32,811 --> 00:02:34,968
assistant manager's assertion.

58
00:02:34,968 --> 00:02:36,740
However, if it is made known ...

59
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:38,589
beforehand, then we may find ...

60
00:02:38,589 --> 00:02:42,364
self-selection arising and distorting the true effect as ...

61
00:02:42,364 --> 00:02:45,754
customers may enter with the sole intent of getting a ...

62
00:02:45,754 --> 00:02:47,757
product from the discount bin.

63
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,763
Other issues arise as well when we are creating ...

64
00:02:53,763 --> 00:02:55,737
experiments. The first is leakages.

65
00:02:55,737 --> 00:02:58,347
Let's suppose we are conducting an experiment on ...

66
00:02:58,347 --> 00:03:00,575
price discounts at a local grocery store.

67
00:03:00,575 --> 00:03:02,357
This will be our treatment group.

68
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,157
To see if these discounts work we need to compare ...

69
00:03:06,157 --> 00:03:09,867
sales with another grocery store that isn't running the ...

70
00:03:09,867 --> 00:03:12,866
price discounts. The issue we face is that our ...

71
00:03:12,866 --> 00:03:16,418
control store may be in too close of proximity and some ...

72
00:03:16,418 --> 00:03:20,838
customers visit that store for the same goods for whatever reason.

73
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:25,244
If we worry about leakages, then we will want to choose a ...

74
00:03:25,244 --> 00:03:26,679
store further away.

75
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:30,560
Another issue that arises is representativeness.

76
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:34,737
Our grocery store want to make sure that their sales ...

77
00:03:34,737 --> 00:03:36,863
can be closely tied to an area,

78
00:03:36,863 --> 00:03:40,861
so they choose another local store (perhaps owned by the ...

79
00:03:40,861 --> 00:03:43,668
same company) to use as a control group.

80
00:03:43,668 --> 00:03:46,985
But as we just learned this may cause leakages.

81
00:03:46,985 --> 00:03:49,877
In the end, we are faced with a trade-off.

82
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:53,385
Back to our endcap display. We now have another ...

83
00:03:53,385 --> 00:03:56,279
endcap that looks very close to the discounted one.

84
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:59,392
We've also lowered the discount to 10%.

85
00:03:59,392 --> 00:04:02,408
We satisfied the representativeness problem ...

86
00:04:02,408 --> 00:04:06,043
by using another endcap as a control that is very close to ...

87
00:04:06,043 --> 00:04:07,358
the discounted one.

88
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,611
But we may have introduced a leakage problem as the ...

89
00:04:11,611 --> 00:04:13,244
10% may not be enough to ...

90
00:04:13,244 --> 00:04:16,941
incentivize customers to purchase from the endcap ...

91
00:04:16,941 --> 00:04:19,176
and surrounding items. Again,

92
00:04:19,176 --> 00:04:24,077
we are faced with a trade-off, and our estimates may not be unbiased.

93
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:28,298
What if we can't generate a randomized experiment?

94
00:04:28,298 --> 00:04:30,842
Sometimes, we have to rely on historical ...

95
00:04:30,842 --> 00:04:33,760
data and data that we can collect after we make a ...

96
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,005
change. When this is our approach we ...

97
00:04:36,005 --> 00:04:38,998
can use the difference-in-difference method.

98
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:42,570
This method does allow us to find the effect of an ...

99
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:45,719
experiment through treatment and control groups.

100
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:49,718
But we also have to keep in mind that our treatment is not ...

101
00:04:49,718 --> 00:04:53,097
random and we can't control for confounders or a variable ...

102
00:04:53,097 --> 00:04:56,476
that effects the dependent and independent variables at ...

103
00:04:56,476 --> 00:04:58,095
the same time. For example,

104
00:04:58,095 --> 00:05:00,770
we want to know the outcome of sales of turkey,

105
00:05:00,770 --> 00:05:02,741
which is our dependent variable,

106
00:05:02,741 --> 00:05:04,712
when we lower the price of turkey,

107
00:05:04,712 --> 00:05:06,824
which is our independent variable.

108
00:05:06,824 --> 00:05:09,640
If we begin this experiment near Thanksgiving,

109
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:12,878
then we may learn that historically prices fall during ...

110
00:05:12,878 --> 00:05:15,553
Thanksgiving and that sales increase during ...

111
00:05:15,553 --> 00:05:18,017
Thanksgiving, so we cannot determine the ...

112
00:05:18,017 --> 00:05:21,677
true causal relationship without controlling for a holiday.

113
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:25,047
Lastly, we have selection bias again.

114
00:05:25,047 --> 00:05:25,874
This, again,

115
00:05:25,874 --> 00:05:29,109
distorts our true effect. With these assumptions,

116
00:05:29,109 --> 00:05:31,667
we can use difference-in-difference to ...

117
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:33,698
estimate the effect of a change.

118
00:05:33,698 --> 00:05:35,729
To do this, we use this equation,

119
00:05:35,729 --> 00:05:38,513
which says the outcome effect is equal to the ...

120
00:05:38,513 --> 00:05:41,673
difference of the treatment group post change and ...

121
00:05:41,673 --> 00:05:45,209
treatment group pre-change minus the difference of the ...

122
00:05:45,209 --> 00:05:48,068
control group post-change and control group ...

123
00:05:48,068 --> 00:05:50,475
pre-change. Let's see how this works.

124
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:54,791
We have a scenario given to us where a grocery store is ...

125
00:05:54,791 --> 00:05:57,789
implementing a new advertising campaign and ...

126
00:05:57,789 --> 00:06:00,944
they want to know how well this campaign worked.

127
00:06:00,944 --> 00:06:04,178
They have data going back 12 months and forward 12 ...

128
00:06:04,178 --> 00:06:06,544
months. They compare their sales to ...

129
00:06:06,544 --> 00:06:08,516
similar stores in the region.

130
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:13,878
We find that the store earned $120,000 in sales ...

131
00:06:13,878 --> 00:06:18,392
post-campaign and $100,000 pre-campaign.

132
00:06:18,392 --> 00:06:23,638
Similar stores earned $115,000 post-campaign and ...

133
00:06:23,638 --> 00:06:26,200
$110,000 pre-campaign.

134
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:29,724
To estimate the effect, let's first recall the outcome ...

135
00:06:29,724 --> 00:06:32,559
effect of the difference-in-difference estimator

136
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,535
Next, let's plug our pre and post ...

137
00:06:36,535 --> 00:06:39,199
numbers into their corresponding spots.

138
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,000
Solving down and we get.

139
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:47,458
$15,000 as the total effect to include our time-varying and ...

140
00:06:47,458 --> 00:06:51,280
constant factors. This tells us we could expect ...

141
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:54,816
a $5,000 change already with no ad campaign.

142
00:06:54,816 --> 00:06:57,969
And by having the ad campaign we added an ...

143
00:06:57,969 --> 00:07:00,358
additional $10,000 in sales.

144
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:05,157
We'll conclude by placing the previous numbers in a table.

145
00:07:05,157 --> 00:07:08,865
This table sorts out the control and treatment groups ...

146
00:07:08,865 --> 00:07:11,749
sales as well as adding in two new columns.

147
00:07:11,749 --> 00:07:15,292
The factors constant over time measures the change ...

148
00:07:15,292 --> 00:07:17,681
from the treatment group. That is,

149
00:07:17,681 --> 00:07:21,059
we are looking at how the group changes by itself.

150
00:07:21,059 --> 00:07:24,602
Subtracting the post treatment and pre-treatment ...

151
00:07:24,602 --> 00:07:25,755
yields $20,000.

152
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,960
And the time-varying factors measures the control group.

153
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:34,337
This allows us to see what would have happened had ...

154
00:07:34,337 --> 00:07:37,159
nothing else taken place over time.

155
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:43,000
Subtracting the post control and pre-control yields $5,000.

156
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:48,039
We take the difference of these two measures to get $15,000.

157
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:49,539
Alright, that's it.

158
00:07:49,539 --> 00:07:52,079
Hope you've enjoyed it and we'll catch you in the next one.
